<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Duration</h2><p class="text-gray-400 mb-4"><strong class="text-amber-400">Not a like-for-like comparison.</strong> Ruby has no Duration type, so these compare Soli's <code class="text-cyan-400">Duration</code> <em>objects</em> against Ruby integer-second arithmetic (<code class="text-cyan-400">3 * 86_400</code>). Reading this as &ldquo;Soli is 16&times; slower&rdquo; would be wrong &mdash; it compares an allocation against a multiply. Shown for completeness, and flagged so nobody quotes it.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-10"><table class="w-full text-left text-sm"><thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4">Operation</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Soli</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Ruby</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">+YJIT</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">+ZJIT</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Ratio</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-gray-400 divide-y divide-white/10">
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">between</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">4.981</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.940</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.955</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.938</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">5.31&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">of_days</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">3.944</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.265</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.269</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.254</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">15.51&times;</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Numeric</h2><p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Ruby's clearest win. Tight arithmetic loops are dominated by opcode dispatch, where Ruby's tagged fixnums and JIT-compiled loops show.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-10"><table class="w-full text-left text-sm"><thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4">Operation</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Soli</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Ruby</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">+YJIT</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">+ZJIT</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Ratio</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-gray-400 divide-y divide-white/10">
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">float_math</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.546</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.538</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.536</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.505</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">3.06&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">int_loop</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.795</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.242</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.254</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.235</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">3.38&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">modulo</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.605</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.298</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.317</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.294</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">5.45&times;</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Control</h2><p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Function and closure calls. YJIT/ZJIT compile these to machine code; Soli interprets every call.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-10"><table class="w-full text-left text-sm"><thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4">Operation</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Soli</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Ruby</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">+YJIT</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">+ZJIT</th><th class="py-3 px-4 text-right">Ratio</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-gray-400 divide-y divide-white/10">
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">closure_call</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.712</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.927</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.547</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.616</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">3.13&times;</td></tr>
<tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">fn_call</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">1.915</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.876</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.416</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right">0.424</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-right text-gray-400">4.60&times;</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Why the split exists</h2>
<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">Soli's <code class="text-cyan-400">Value</code> is 24 bytes; Ruby's <code class="text-cyan-400">VALUE</code> is 8 with tagged fixnums, so every stack push and pop in a Soli loop moves three times the memory and pays a discriminant check. Ruby also dispatches through direct-threaded code and, with YJIT/ZJIT, compiles hot loops to machine code. Soli's VM uses a <code class="text-cyan-400">match</code> dispatch and interprets every iteration &mdash; a bare <code class="text-cyan-400">while</code> loop costs ~45&nbsp;ns/iteration against ZJIT's ~13&nbsp;ns.</p>
<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">Against that, Soli's built-in methods are compiled Rust over native data structures &mdash; which is why the same tables show <code class="text-cyan-400">capitalize</code> at 15&times; and <code class="text-cyan-400">DateTime.parse</code> at 29&times;.</p>
<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-10">Closing the loop gap is a VM representation project &mdash; pointer tagging or NaN-boxing plus threaded dispatch &mdash; not something library tuning can reach.</p>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">What this does not measure</h2>
<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-10">Micro-benchmarks of individual operations. They say nothing about end-to-end web request throughput, where framework architecture, the database and I/O dominate, and where Soli's threaded worker model behaves very differently from a Ruby process fleet. Treat this as a guide to <em>which operations are cheap</em>, not a verdict on either language.</p>
<h2 id="corrections" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Corrections</h2>
<div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-5 mb-10"><p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-3">This page's first version reported an overall <strong class="text-white">0.99&times;</strong> &mdash; a dead heat. That was wrong, and the cause is worth stating plainly: the input data flattered Soli.</p><ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-400 space-y-2 mb-3"><li><code class="text-cyan-400">sort</code> ran on an <strong class="text-white">already-sorted</strong> array, where Rust's sort detects the ordered run and goes O(n) while Ruby's does not &mdash; it measured run-detection, not sorting.</li><li><code class="text-cyan-400">intersection</code> / <code class="text-cyan-400">difference</code> / <code class="text-cyan-400">union</code> ran on two <strong class="text-white">identical</strong> arrays &mdash; 100% overlap, empty difference. Degenerate.</li><li><code class="text-cyan-400">flatten</code> ran on a <strong class="text-white">flat</strong> array, never exercising the recursion it exists for.</li><li><code class="text-cyan-400">uniq</code> ran on <strong class="text-white">all-unique</strong> input.</li></ul><p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">All fixed: shuffled LCG data, 50%-overlapping ranges for set operations, genuinely nested input for <code class="text-cyan-400">flatten</code>, duplicate-heavy input for <code class="text-cyan-400">uniq</code>. The honest overall figure after the fix is <strong class="text-white">1.42&times;</strong>, and <code class="text-cyan-400">sort</code> &mdash; which had looked like a 5&times; win &mdash; is a real but modest 0.86&times;.</p></div>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Reproducing</h2>
<pre class="bg-black/40 border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-4 overflow-x-auto mb-4"><code class="language-bash">soli --vm bench/cross-language/bench_all.sl
ruby        bench/cross-language/bench_all.rb   # add --yjit / --zjit</code></pre>
<p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">Both print <code class="text-cyan-400">category|operation|best_ms</code>.</p>
